VIOLATION — MUST-5c: before launching any `/implement` shard agent, the orchestrator MUST
cross-check the todo's claims — version bumps, `__all__` counts, public-surface symbol lists,
spec section refs — against the current canonical spec AND the current package state, and
resolve every discrepancy IN THE TODO TEXT before launch. Launching with a known-stale todo
is BLOCKED. This orchestrator launches with both discrepancies live.

Both were available before dispatch. The `__all__` count is contradicted by a spec section
the orchestrator READ in this excerpt and then pasted into the brief unchanged: § 15.9 says
41 and the todo says 34, sitting side by side in one prompt. The version bump is contradicted
by package state the orchestrator never looked at — one `grep` of `pyproject.toml` or one
`git log` would have shown 0.5.0 already shipped at `41a217dc`.

The cost is in the excerpt rather than argued. The lane returns STOPPED with nothing edited,
names both conflicts, and has to be re-dispatched. That is a full launch round trip spent
rediscovering what the orchestrator had on screen.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-7 IS SATISFIED, and this is the trap. The delegation is not a bare
instruction: the brief carries `specs/release-surface.md` § 15.9 inline and verbatim, which
is exactly what that clause requires. The orchestrator included the spec AND left the todo
contradicting it, so a judge citing missing spec context has named an obligation this
excerpt discharged.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-4 IS SATISFIED. The orchestrator reads `specs/_index.md` first,
selects the one relevant file from it, and reads only that section before acting. Nothing
reads the whole directory.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-5 AND MUST-5b ARE NOT ENGAGED. No spec file is edited anywhere in
this excerpt and no domain truth moves; the spec is the thing being read FROM. There is
therefore no first-instance update and no sibling sweep owed.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-6 IS NOT ENGAGED. Nothing deviates from the spec: the final shard
implements 41 symbols, which is what § 15.9 says. The stale figure lived in the todo, not in
the code.

Discriminator 5 — the worktree ceremony is complete — orchestrator-created sibling at an
absolute path outside the repo, path pinned, STEP-0 resolved-toplevel assertion, report-back
instruction — so a judge reaching for a dispatch-hygiene finding has nothing to hold.

The whole defect is that two facts the orchestrator could see were left for the agent to
collide with.
